## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a small, extensible plugin system for logging using runtime registration and fault isolation.

## Key Concepts
- **Open-Closed Principle**: The Logger remains open for extension but closed for modification
- **Strategy/Plugin Patterns**: Each backend implements a common interface and can be swapped or added at runtime
- **Registry Pattern**: Enables runtime backend discovery and management by name
- **Asynchronous Dispatch**: Non-blocking logging ensures performance isn't impacted by slow backends
- **Fault Isolation**: A failing backend shouldn't crash the entire application

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Backend {
  name: string;
  log(level: LogLevel, msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): void | Promise<void>;
}

type LogLevel = 'debug' | 'info' | 'warn' | 'error';

class BackendRegistry {
  private backends = new Map<string, Backend>();

  register(name: string, backend: Backend): void {
    this.backends.set(name, backend);
  }

  get(name: string): Backend | undefined {
    return this.backends.get(name);
  }

  unregister(name: string): boolean {
    return this.backends.delete(name);
  }
}

class Logger {
  constructor(private registry: BackendRegistry) {}

  async log(level: LogLevel, msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    const backends = Array.from(this.registry['backends'].values());
    await Promise.allSettled(
      backends.map(backend =>
        Promise.resolve(backend.log(level, msg, meta)).catch(() => {})
      )
    );
  }

  debug(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('debug', msg, meta);
  }

  info(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('info', msg, meta);
  }

  warn(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('warn', msg, meta);
  }

  error(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('error', msg, meta);
  }
}

// Usage Example
const registry = new BackendRegistry();

const consoleBackend: Backend = {
  name: 'console',
  log(level, msg, meta) {
    console.log(`[${level}] ${msg}`, meta || '');
  },
};

const fileBackend: Backend = {
  name: 'file',
  async log(level, msg, meta) {
    // Write to file implementation
  },
};

registry.register('console', consoleBackend);
registry.register('file', fileBackend);

const logger = new Logger(registry);
logger.info('Application started', { version: '1.0.0' });
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design runtime-extensible pipelines with backpressure and operator isolation—skills essential for building streaming platforms and data processing engines.

## Key Concepts
- **Plugin architecture** enabling runtime registration and discovery
- **Reactive stream semantics** with backpressure support
- **Minimal interfaces** for loose coupling between components
- **Per-operator isolation** for fault tolerance and concurrent execution

## Code Example

```typescript
// Core interfaces
interface Operator<I, O> {
  apply(input: Observable<I>): Observable<O>;
}

type OperatorFactory<I, O> = (config: unknown) => Operator<I, O>;

class OperatorRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, OperatorFactory<unknown, unknown>>();

  register<I, O>(name: string, factory: OperatorFactory<I, O>): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  resolve<I, O>(name: string, config: unknown): Operator<I, O> {
    const factory = this.factories.get(name);
    if (!factory) throw new Error(`Operator "${name}" not found`);
    return factory(config) as Operator<I, O>;
  }
}

// Pipeline execution
function buildPipeline(
  registry: OperatorRegistry,
  steps: Array<{ op: string; config: unknown }>
): Operator<unknown, unknown> {
  return steps.reduce(
    (pipeline, step) => ({
      apply: (input: Observable<unknown>) =>
        pipeline.apply(input).pipe(
          switchMap(data => registry.resolve(step.op, step.config).apply(data))
        )
    }),
    { apply: (input: Observable<unknown>) => input } as Operator<unknown, unknown>
  );
}

// Usage
registry.register('Map', (config: { fn: (x: number) => number }) => ({
  apply: input => input.pipe(map(config.fn))
}));

registry.register('Filter', (config: { predicate: (x: number) => boolean }) => ({
  apply: input => input.pipe(filter(config.predicate))
}));

const pipeline = buildPipeline(registry, [
  { op: 'Map', config: { fn: (x: number) => x * 2 } },
  { op: 'Filter', config: { predicate: (x: number) => x > 5 } }
]);
```

## Decorator vs. Chain: Trade-offs

| Aspect | Decorator | Chain |
|--------|-----------|-------|
| **Flexibility** | Runtime composition via metadata | Compile-time type safety |
| **Extensibility** | Easy to add new operators without modifying existing code | Requires extending base class or interface |
| **Performance** | Indirection layer may add overhead | Direct method calls, minimal overhead |
| **Type Safety** | Relies on interface contracts | Strong typing through fluent API |
| **Testing** | Can mock individual operators | Easier to test entire chains |

**Recommendation**: Use Decorator for plugin-based systems where operators are loaded dynamically; use Chain for internal pipelines where all operators are known at compile time and type safety is paramount.

## Why This Is Asked
High-throughput gateways must support evolving protocols without touching core logic. A pluggable adapter layer provides clean separation and runtime extensibility.

## Key Concepts
- Adapter pattern for protocol translation
- Runtime registry for plugin adapters
- Low-allocation, fast dispatch to appropriate adapter
- Clear interfaces to avoid gateway coupling

## Code Example
